    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-feb-calculus-2002-p8.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(u=\\sqrt{x^{2}+x}\\). Then \\(d u=\\frac{2 x+1}{2 \\sqrt{x^{2}+x}} d x\\). So the integral becomes \\(2 \\int \\frac{d u}{\\left(4 x^{2}+4 x+1\\right)}\\), or \\(2 \\int \\frac{d u}{4 u^{2}+1}\\). This is \\(\\tan ^{-1}(2 u)\\), yielding a final answer of \\(\\tan ^{-1}\\left(2 \\sqrt{x^{2}+x}\\right)+C\\) for the indefinite integral. The definite integral becomes \\(\\tan ^{-1}(1)-\\tan ^{-1}(0)=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\).",
